    "    {\"q\": \"Explain the Pearson correlation coefficient.\",\n",
    "     \"a\": \"Pearson r measures linear relationship strength between two continuous variables. Range: -1 to +1. +1 = perfect positive linear, -1 = perfect negative linear, 0 = no linear relationship. Assumes normality, linearity, homoscedasticity.\"},\n",
    "    {\"q\": \"When would you use Spearman vs Pearson correlation?\",\n",
    "     \"a\": \"Pearson for linear relationships with normally distributed continuous variables. Spearman (rank correlation) for monotonic non-linear relationships, ordinal data, or when outliers distort Pearson.\"},\n",
    "    {\"q\": \"What is a box plot and what does it show?\",\n",
    "     \"a\": \"Box plot (box-and-whisker) shows distribution via quartiles: box = IQR (Q1 to Q3), line = median, whiskers = 1.5×IQR, points beyond = outliers. Reveals central tendency, spread, skewness, and outliers at a glance.\"},\n",
    "    {\"q\": \"How do you detect outliers in a dataset?\",\n",
